{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 30,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the basic arrangement: Xenophon provided public-relations support for anticipated litigation, and the law firm resisted flat monthly charges, particularly after directing Xenophon to stop work."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The distinction between a flat monthly fee and an advance retainer drawn against billable hours is explained in the opinion, without requiring tobacco-litigation or communications-industry expertise."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding section 4.1 requires reading the invoicing provisions alongside section 1.2's additional-services rates, section 2.2's termination notice, and section 6.1's stop-work right."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The court expressly adopts the flat-fee construction and explains its supporting readings, while clearly declining to reach extrinsic-evidence and conditional contract-formation arguments."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The whole-contract construction in d04 overlaps with the additional-services, invoicing, and termination constructions, and d02/d08 and d05/d07 describe shared reasoning from different provisions."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d04 — The reviewer must integrate the hours-tracking and retrospective invoicing language with the additional-services and termination provisions to understand why the court treated the retainer as a flat fee. This overarching construction must also be distinguished from the narrower provision-specific defects that supply its reasoning.",
  "reason": "This is a straightforward payment dispute on Xenophon's motion for summary judgment, not a case requiring specialised industry knowledge. The principal interpretive task is to connect several short provisions and understand why recording billable time did not make compensation hourly. A reviewer should also distinguish stopping work from terminating the agreement, and recognise the court's treatment of continued staff availability as services during the remaining term. The overlapping descriptions warrant a careful cross-check, but the court's express holdings make this suitable for a general contract reviewer rather than a specialist."
}
